From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: sun6i-rtc: a733 LOSC half on a split RTC block

On our boot chain the A733's LOSC runs from the internal RC, not from the
32.768 kHz crystal the board carries. Measured on both boards,
2026-09-17 and 2026-09-22: LOSC_CTRL (0x07090000) = 0x0000401c,
LOSC_AUTO_SWT_STA (0x07090004) = 0. LOSC_SRC_SEL = 0 selects the 16 MHz RC
(UM V1.00 9.1.7.1), divided by 32 and by INTOSC_CLK_PRESCAL + 1 =
16 (9.1.7.3, 0x0008 reads 0xf): 31250 Hz, 4.6 % slow before the RC's own
tolerance. The RTC timekeeper (`arm64-dts-a733-add-the-rtc-timekeeper`) lost 3 s in 48 s on it.

Mainline already has the mechanism: ccu-sun6i-rtc models LOSC_SRC_SEL as the
"osc32k" mux with the 0x16AA key, and the crystal as an "ext-osc32k" input
behind the EXT_LOSC_EN gate; a board selects it through assigned-clock-rates
on CLK_OSC32K (the sun55i boards). rtc-sun6i calls it for the RTC node on its
own mapping. This patch adds the A733 match data. `arm64-dts-a733-run-losc-from-the-32k-crystal` wires the DT.

== The register, from the UM

  LOSC_CTRL_REG 0x0000 (9.1.7.1)
    [31:16] KEY_FIELD         0x16AA, required to write bits 1:0
    [15]    AUTO_SWT_FUNCTION 0 = auto switch enabled
    [14]    AUTO_SWT_32K_SEL  1 = a lost crystal sends LOSC back to the RC
    [4]     EXT_LOSC_EN       crystal enable, reset value 1
    [3:2]   EXT_LOSC_GSM      drive; left alone, changing it is not advised
    [1]     RTC_SRC_SEL       0 = LOSC_SRC, 1 = DCXO/N 32k
    [0]     LOSC_SRC_SEL      0 = RC16M/32/N, 1 = external 32.768 kHz
  LOSC_AUTO_SWT_STA_REG 0x0004 (9.1.7.2)
    [2] EXT_LOSC_STA (1 = crystal abnormal, valid with auto switch on),
    [1] AUTO_SWT_PEND (W1C), [0] LOSC_SRC_SEL_STA; [31:3] reserved

0x401c decodes as: auto switch on with RC fallback (15:14 = 01), crystal
enabled (4), GSM high (3:2 = 11, set by the firmware; the reset value is
0x4010), RTC counter on LOSC (1 = 0), LOSC from the RC (0 = 0). The crystal
runs and is monitored; only the mux is on the wrong input. The vendor stack
reads 0x401d, STA 0x1 -- the same word with bit 0 set.

UM 9.1.5.1 gives two procedures. (c), with the auto switch: bit 14 = 1,
crystal on (bit 4), bit 15 = 0, EXT_LOSC_STA read three times with 0 meaning
the crystal started, then select. Its steps 5 and 7 as printed write RTC_SRC_SEL = 1
and LOSC_SRC_SEL = 0, which select DCXO/N and the RC -- apparently a misprint. (d), without
the auto switch: bit 15 = 1, bit 14 = 0, then bit 0 = 1. Common to both, and
all we need: the crystal is enabled before bit 0 is set. The auto switch
stays as the firmware left it, on, which is (c)'s state; its startup check is
moot here because bit 4 has been 1 since reset and STA reads 0 (bit 2 clear,
crystal normal) before the switch. We check the result afterwards instead.

The clock framework produces that order unchanged: reparenting "osc32k" to
"ext-osc32k-gate" first enables the gate (LOSC_CTRL |= BIT(4), no key -- the
key guards bits 1:0 only), then ccu_mux writes 0x16aa0000 | bit 0. From
0x401c the two writes are 0x0000401c and 0x16aa401d. With the auto switch on,
a crystal that does not run is caught by the hardware, which returns bit 0 to
the RC and sets AUTO_SWT_PEND.

== What the A733 entry leaves out, and why

Our DT splits the RTC block (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-maps-only-the-dcxo-control-group`): the RTC node maps 0x000-0x15b, and the
DCXO control group 0x15c-0x16f is the A733 RTC-CCU (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-minimal-rtc-ccu` and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo`). Mainline's
code touches that group in two places:

 - "iosc" gates CLK16M_RC_EN, XO_CTRL0 (0x160) bit 0. On the A733 that
   register is behind the XO_CTRL_WP_REG (0x15c) 0x16AA key (9.1.7.15), which
   the gate does not write, and the RC is the auto switch's fallback. So the
   A733 iosc keeps rate and accuracy and gets no gate ops (dcxo_ctrl_elsewhere):
   the RC stays as the firmware left it, on (0x160 bit 0 = 1 on both boards).
 - "osc24M-32k" (the DCXO/N 32k) is not registered. Its A733 divider follows
   the DCXO_ST strap in XO_CTRL0 [15:14] (upstream reads it there, see below).
   Nothing selects it (RTC_SRC_SEL = 0), so rtc-32k gets one parent, as on
   the H616, and the fanout mux the two inputs it can take here.

No IOSC calibration: INTOSC_CLK_AUTO_CALI_REG (0x000c) reads 0x7a000000, off,
on both boards. With it on, ccu_iosc_32k_prepare() would switch the RC path
to the calibrated output at registration, and iosc-32k would report 32768 Hz
like the crystal; assigned-clock-rates would then pick a parent by which rate
was cached first. Without it the RC path is 31250 Hz and only the crystal
matches 32768.

Diagnostics: after the provider is registered -- when of_clk_add_hw_provider()
has applied the node's assigned rate -- the A733 path reads LOSC_CTRL and
polls LOSC_SRC_SEL_STA for up to 10 ms, and logs which oscillator the hardware
reports. A crystal that was selected but not confirmed, or EXT_LOSC_STA set,
is a warning. That line is the board check for this patch.

== Upstream

"clk: sunxi-ng: sun6i-rtc: add a733 support" (0a136efc0fc2, 2026-07-23, after
6.18) models the whole 0x400 block in one node: the LOSC half as here, plus
the DCXO-strap divider for osc24M-32k at 0x160 and the four DCXO gates at
0x16c as CLK_HOSC_*. Its data sets have_iosc_calibration. We do not take it
as it stands: its 0x160/0x16c clocks would be a second provider for
registers our RTC-CCU owns -- and an unused hosc-serdes0 there would be gated
by clk_disable_unused under COMB0's feet. The LOSC mux, the key and the
ext-osc32k gate are mainline's own and unchanged; what differs is scoped to
the split. Moving to upstream's one-node shape replaces `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-minimal-rtc-ccu`, `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-add-dcxo` and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-rtc-ccu-maps-only-the-dcxo-control-group` and
every rtc_ccu consumer; that stays open.

== What the vendor does and leaves out

The BSP's ccu-sun60iw2-rtc.c switches in clock_source_init() at probe:
forces LOSC_CTRL [15:14] = 01, enables the crystal only if bit 4 is clear,
then polls LOSC_AUTO_SWT_STA with mask 0x10 for "stable" and sets bit 0.
0x10 is bit 4, reserved in STA; EXT_LOSC_STA is bit 2 (9.1.7.2). Bit 0 is set
whatever the poll returns, and nothing reads LOSC_SRC_SEL_STA afterwards.
Its RC path is a fixed factor of 500 ("iosc-div32k", 32000 Hz); the divider
is 32 * (N + 1) = 512 at the reset prescaler, 31250 Hz. We take none of it:
the switch is mainline's mux, and the readback checks bit 0 of STA.
We do not force [15:14]: both boards read 01, and mainline does not own
them. The logged LOSC_CTRL shows them.

== Verification

Applies at fuzz 0; compile-check. Not on silicon. What proves it (with `arm64-dts-a733-run-losc-from-the-32k-crystal`):
  dmesg        "LOSC from the external 32768 Hz crystal (LOSC_CTRL 0x0000401d,
               STA 0x00000001)"
  devmem       0x07090000 = 0x401d, 0x07090004 bit 0 = 1
  clk_summary  osc32k 32768 under ext-osc32k-gate / ext_osc32k; iosc 16000000
  RTC vs NTP   ~0 drift over 10 min instead of -4.6 %
Falsifier: the warning line, STA bit 0 = 0, or a drift that stays at -4.6 %.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun6i-rtc.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun6i-rtc.c
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@
 #include <linux/clk-provider.h>
 #include <linux/device.h>
 #include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/iopoll.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/of_device.h>
@@ -29,6 +30,11 @@
 
 #define LOSC_CTRL_REG			0x0
 #define LOSC_CTRL_KEY			0x16aa0000
+#define LOSC_CTRL_SRC_SEL_EXT		BIT(0)
+
+#define LOSC_AUTO_SWT_STA_REG		0x4
+#define LOSC_AUTO_SWT_STA_SRC_EXT	BIT(0)
+#define LOSC_AUTO_SWT_STA_EXT_BAD	BIT(2)
 
 #define IOSC_32K_CLK_DIV_REG		0x8
 #define IOSC_32K_CLK_DIV		GENMASK(4, 0)
@@ -48,6 +54,8 @@
 	bool				have_ext_osc32k		: 1;
 	bool				have_iosc_calibration	: 1;
 	bool				rtc_32k_single_parent	: 1;
+	bool				have_losc_auto_swt_sta	: 1;
+	bool				dcxo_ctrl_elsewhere	: 1;
 	const struct clk_parent_data	*osc32k_fanout_parents;
 	u8				osc32k_fanout_nparents;
 };
@@ -108,10 +116,24 @@
 	.recalc_accuracy	= ccu_iosc_recalc_accuracy,
 };
 
+/*
+ * For SoCs whose RC16M enable is not in this device's register window: the
+ * rate and accuracy only, no gate. See sun60i_a733_rtc_ccu_data.
+ */
+static const struct clk_ops ccu_iosc_rate_ops = {
+	.recalc_rate		= ccu_iosc_recalc_rate,
+	.recalc_accuracy	= ccu_iosc_recalc_accuracy,
+};
+
+static struct clk_init_data iosc_init_data = {
+	.name		= "iosc",
+	.ops		= &ccu_iosc_ops,	/* updated during probe */
+	.flags		= CLK_GET_RATE_NOCACHE,
+};
+
 static struct ccu_common iosc_clk = {
 	.reg		= DCXO_CTRL_REG,
-	.hw.init	= CLK_HW_INIT_NO_PARENT("iosc", &ccu_iosc_ops,
-						CLK_GET_RATE_NOCACHE),
+	.hw.init	= &iosc_init_data,
 };
 
 static int ccu_iosc_32k_prepare(struct clk_hw *hw)
@@ -312,6 +334,16 @@
 	{ .hw = &osc24M_32k_clk.common.hw }
 };
 
+/*
+ * The A733's RTC_32K fanout mux (CLK32K_FOUT_CTRL_GATING_REG [2:1], UM V1.00
+ * 9.1.7.13): 00 RTC_32K, 01 LOSC, 10 DCXO divided 32K. The third input is not
+ * registered here (see below), so the mux offers the first two.
+ */
+static const struct clk_parent_data sun60i_a733_osc32k_fanout_parents[] = {
+	{ .hw = &osc32k_clk.common.hw },
+	{ .hw = &ext_osc32k_gate_clk.common.hw },
+};
+
 static const struct sun6i_rtc_match_data sun50i_h616_rtc_ccu_data = {
 	.have_iosc_calibration	= true,
 	.rtc_32k_single_parent	= true,
@@ -332,6 +364,29 @@
 	.osc32k_fanout_nparents	= ARRAY_SIZE(sun50i_r329_osc32k_fanout_parents),
 };
 
+/*
+ * A733: the LOSC half of the RTC block only. The DCXO control group
+ * (XO_CTRL_WP_REG 0x15c to XO_CTRL1_REG 0x16c) is a separate clock provider on
+ * this SoC and outside this device's register window, so nothing here may
+ * touch DCXO_CTRL_REG (0x160):
+ *  - "iosc" keeps its rate but has no gate. CLK16M_RC_EN is XO_CTRL0 bit 0,
+ *    behind the XO_CTRL_WP_REG key (UM 9.1.7.15/16), and the RC must keep
+ *    running as the hardware fallback of the LOSC auto switch (9.1.5.1 c).
+ *  - "osc24M-32k" is not registered: its divider follows the DCXO_ST strap in
+ *    XO_CTRL0 [15:14], so rtc-32k gets the one parent it uses (RTC_SRC_SEL = 0).
+ * No IOSC calibration: 0x000c reads 0x7a000000 (off) on both boards, and the
+ * calibrated RC would report 32768 Hz as well, which would make the
+ * assigned-clock-rates choice between RC and crystal depend on cache timing.
+ */
+static const struct sun6i_rtc_match_data sun60i_a733_rtc_ccu_data = {
+	.have_ext_osc32k	= true,
+	.rtc_32k_single_parent	= true,
+	.have_losc_auto_swt_sta	= true,
+	.dcxo_ctrl_elsewhere	= true,
+	.osc32k_fanout_parents	= sun60i_a733_osc32k_fanout_parents,
+	.osc32k_fanout_nparents	= ARRAY_SIZE(sun60i_a733_osc32k_fanout_parents),
+};
+
 static const struct of_device_id sun6i_rtc_ccu_match[] = {
 	{
 		.compatible	= "allwinner,sun50i-h616-rtc",
@@ -345,15 +400,49 @@
 		.compatible	= "allwinner,sun55i-a523-rtc",
 		.data		= &sun55i_a523_rtc_ccu_data,
 	},
+	{
+		.compatible	= "allwinner,sun60i-a733-rtc",
+		.data		= &sun60i_a733_rtc_ccu_data,
+	},
 	{},
 };
 MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, sun6i_rtc_ccu_match);
 
+/*
+ * Report which oscillator LOSC runs from once the provider is registered, and
+ * with it any assigned-clock-rates of the node applied. LOSC_SRC_SEL_STA
+ * (LOSC_AUTO_SWT_STA_REG bit 0, UM 9.1.7.2) is the hardware's answer, not the
+ * bit that was written: with the auto switch enabled, a crystal that does not
+ * run sends LOSC back to the RC and clears it.
+ */
+static void sun6i_rtc_ccu_report_losc(struct device *dev, void __iomem *reg)
+{
+	u32 ctrl, sta;
+	int ret;
+
+	ctrl = readl(reg + LOSC_CTRL_REG);
+	if (!(ctrl & LOSC_CTRL_SRC_SEL_EXT)) {
+		dev_info(dev, "LOSC from the internal RC (LOSC_CTRL %#010x, STA %#010x)\n",
+			 ctrl, readl(reg + LOSC_AUTO_SWT_STA_REG));
+		return;
+	}
+
+	ret = readl_poll_timeout(reg + LOSC_AUTO_SWT_STA_REG, sta,
+				 sta & LOSC_AUTO_SWT_STA_SRC_EXT, 10, 10000);
+	if (ret || (sta & LOSC_AUTO_SWT_STA_EXT_BAD))
+		dev_warn(dev, "LOSC: external crystal selected but not confirmed (LOSC_CTRL %#010x, STA %#010x)\n",
+			 readl(reg + LOSC_CTRL_REG), sta);
+	else
+		dev_info(dev, "LOSC from the external 32768 Hz crystal (LOSC_CTRL %#010x, STA %#010x)\n",
+			 readl(reg + LOSC_CTRL_REG), sta);
+}
+
 int sun6i_rtc_ccu_probe(struct device *dev, void __iomem *reg)
 {
 	const struct sun6i_rtc_match_data *data;
 	struct clk *ext_osc32k_clk = NULL;
 	const struct of_device_id *match;
+	int ret;
 
 	/* This driver is only used for newer variants of the hardware. */
 	match = of_match_device(sun6i_rtc_ccu_match, dev);
@@ -389,7 +478,19 @@
 	osc32k_fanout_init_data.parent_data = data->osc32k_fanout_parents;
 	osc32k_fanout_init_data.num_parents = data->osc32k_fanout_nparents;
 
-	return devm_sunxi_ccu_probe(dev, reg, &sun6i_rtc_ccu_desc);
+	if (data->dcxo_ctrl_elsewhere) {
+		iosc_init_data.ops = &ccu_iosc_rate_ops;
+		sun6i_rtc_ccu_hw_clks.hws[CLK_OSC24M_32K] = NULL;
+	}
+
+	ret = devm_sunxi_ccu_probe(dev, reg, &sun6i_rtc_ccu_desc);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	if (data->have_losc_auto_swt_sta)
+		sun6i_rtc_ccu_report_losc(dev, reg);
+
+	return 0;
 }
 
 MODULE_IMPORT_NS("SUNXI_CCU");
